Morris traversal uses temporary right links to walk the tree without a stack or recursion. When a node has a left subtree, its inorder predecessor temporarily points back to that node. The temporary link is removed before the node is processed.

This is an in-place version and changes the tree pointers into list pointers. A null root returns null, and the final node is explicitly given a null right pointer.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    TreeNode* bToDLL(TreeNode* root) {
        TreeNode* current = root;
        TreeNode* head = nullptr;
        TreeNode* previous = nullptr;

        while (current != nullptr) {
            if (current->left == nullptr) {
                if (head == nullptr) {
                    head = current;
                } else {
                    previous->right = current;
                    current->left = previous;
                }
                previous = current;
                current = current->right;
            } else {
                TreeNode* predecessor = current->left;

                while (predecessor->right != nullptr &&
                       predecessor->right != current) {
                    predecessor = predecessor->right;
                }

                if (predecessor->right == nullptr) {
                    predecessor->right = current;
                    current = current->left;
                } else {
                    predecessor->right = nullptr;

                    if (head == nullptr) {
                        head = current;
                    } else {
                        previous->right = current;
                        current->left = previous;
                    }
                    previous = current;
                    current = current->right;
                }
            }
        }

        if (head != nullptr) {
            head->left = nullptr;
        }
        if (previous != nullptr) {
            previous->right = nullptr;
        }

        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`. Each temporary link is created and removed once.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the tree nodes.

Each recursive call returns the first and last node of the list made from that subtree. The current root is placed between the converted left and right lists.

A one-node tree returns that node as both its head and tail. For a larger tree, the left tail connects to the root, and the root connects to the right head. This also makes the empty-subtree cases simple.

Dry run for a root with left value 1 and right value 3: the left result is `1`, the right result is `3`, so the root becomes `1 <-> root <-> 3`.

```java
class Solution {
    private TreeNode[] joinSubtree(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return new TreeNode[] {null, null};
        }

        TreeNode[] left = joinSubtree(root.left);
        TreeNode[] right = joinSubtree(root.right);

        if (left[1] != null) {
            left[1].right = root;
            root.left = left[1];
        } else {
            root.left = null;
        }

        if (right[0] != null) {
            root.right = right[0];
            right[0].left = root;
        } else {
            root.right = null;
        }

        TreeNode first = left[0] != null ? left[0] : root;
        TreeNode last = right[1] != null ? right[1] : root;
        return new TreeNode[] {first, last};
    }

    TreeNode bToDLL(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode[] result = joinSubtree(root);
        result[0].left = null;
        result[1].right = null;
        return result[0];
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for recursive calls, plus small arrays returned at each level. The input nodes are reused, not copied.

A stack gives the same inorder order without recursive calls. Each time a node is removed from the stack, it is the next node in the doubly linked list.

The empty tree is handled before the loop. At the end, the first node has no previous node and the last node has no next node.

```java
class Solution {
    TreeNode bToDLL(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return null;
        }

        java.util.ArrayDeque<TreeNode> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        TreeNode current = root;
        TreeNode head = null;
        TreeNode previous = null;

        while (current != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
            while (current != null) {
                stack.push(current);
                current = current.left;
            }

            current = stack.pop();

            if (head == null) {
                head = current;
            } else {
                previous.right = current;
                current.left = previous;
            }
            previous = current;

            current = current.right;
        }

        head.left = null;
        previous.right = null;
        return head;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the stack. The tree nodes themselves are reused, but their pointers are changed.

An inorder walk visits the tree nodes in the order needed for the list. I keep the first visited node as `head` and connect each new node after `previous`.

The links are changed in place, so no new tree nodes are made. For a tree with values 4, 2, 5, 1, 3, the inorder order is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and those become the list links.

```cpp
class Solution {
    TreeNode* head = nullptr;
    TreeNode* previous = nullptr;

    void inorder(TreeNode* node) {
        if (node == nullptr) {
            return;
        }

        inorder(node->left);

        if (previous == nullptr) {
            head = node;
        } else {
            previous->right = node;
            node->left = previous;
        }
        previous = node;

        inorder(node->right);
    }

public:
    TreeNode* bToDLL(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }

        inorder(root);
        previous->right = nullptr;
        head->left = nullptr;
        return head;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` because every node is visited once.
Space complexity: `O(h)` for the recursion stack, where `h` is the tree height.
